1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:04,500
How EcoCycle collects used cooking oil.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:10,300
Thousands of small kitchens in the Pavuna–Meriti basin fry food every day.

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:15,400
Nobody buys the used oil, so it goes down the drain.

4
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:20,400
One litre can contaminate up to 25,000 litres of water.

5
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,400
Inside the pipes it hardens into a fatberg, and the drain blocks.

6
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,700
13,000 unblocking operations a year. The same streets flood every season.

7
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:35,500
The catadores who work these streets live on cardboard, at R$0.15–0.40 a kilo.

8
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:40,700
The oil going past them sells for R$0.80–2.50 a litre.

9
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:44,700
Demand for it grew over 700% in a decade. None of it reaches them.

10
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:49,800
The workers exist. The buyers exist.

11
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:56,400
What is missing is the layer in between — one that collects the oil and proves what it is.

12
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:02,700
One. A shop registers at the residents' association and gets a PET bottle with its own code.

13
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:08,500
Nothing to buy, nothing to pay. Households can join too, for Green Points.

14
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:14,500
Two. A catador from the local cooperative collects at the door, and scans the code.

15
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:20,700
One scan records four things: how much, from where, when, and who collected it.

16
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:26,500
The oil stops being anonymous waste. It has a history.

17
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:32,700
Three. Everything is pooled at a neighbourhood hub, and tested for water and dirt.

18
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:38,500
The batch only ships if the total matches the sum of every scan.

19
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:43,700
Twenty-four small pickups become one verified, contract-grade batch.

20
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:49,700
Four. The batch is sold under contract — to several processors, never just one.

21
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:57,500
PROVE depended on a single refinery. When it shut down in 2026, the collected oil had nowhere to go.

22
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:02,900
Five. The money comes back by a rule fixed in advance.

23
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:09,000
40% to the catadores who collected it; 20% to households; 20% as Green Points in local shops;

24
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:14,600
and 20% to the Faetequinho course, which already trades free classes for used oil.

25
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:19,900
Six. Every scan teaches the system.

26
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:25,700
It forecasts volume by zone, shortens the routes, and flags the shops about to drop out.

27
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:31,500
Two dashboards: one for the city, one for buyers looking for verified supply.

28
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:40,500
Kitchen. Scan. Hub. Contract. Payment. And data that makes the next round cheaper.

29
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:46,100
The Jardim América pilot: 75,678 litres recovered a year.

30
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:52,500
R$113,517 of revenue against R$105,000 of cost — and R$1.27 of social value per R$1 invested.

31
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:56,200
Same oil. A different path.

32
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:59,700
EcoCycle — a collection system for the last mile.
